    "available_at": "2026-11-16T20:00:43.867530+00:00",
    "body": "The 2,488-carat Motswedi diamond. ( Image courtesy of Lucara Diamond. )\nPosted By: Cecilia Jamasmie November 16, 2026\nLucara Diamond (TSX: LUC) has raised its annual revenue forecast by 30% at the midpoint after sales of two giant diamonds from its Karowe mine in Botswana boosted returns amid continued weakness in smaller stones.\nThe Vancouver-based miner now expects full-year revenue of $140 million to $160 million (C$200 million to C$228 million), up from its previous guidance of $100 million to $130 million, Lucara said Nov. 13.\n“Lucara Diamond reported better-than-expected operational metrics, largely due to higher throughput,” BMO Capital Markets mining analyst Raj Ray said in a Nov. 16 note. “Revenue improved due to the sale of the 1,303-carat diamond, hence revenue guidance has been lifted.”\nThe higher revenue outlook and lower operating-cost forecast offer support as Lucara develops Karowe’s underground expansion, which is expected to reach full-scale production in the first half of 2028. The company’s reliance on exceptional stones also leaves quarterly results sensitive to the timing of individual sales.\nThe 2,488-carat Motswedi diamond was sold in the second quarter, while the 1,303-carat stone was recovered and sold in the third quarter. Both contributed to the annual guidance increase.\nThird-quarter revenue rose 19% year on year to $61.1 million, well above BMO’s $33.3-million forecast, as sales volumes increased 12% to 113,227 carats. Net profit slipped 11% to $6.6 million from $7.4 million a year earlier.\nLucara shares gained 25% to C14¢ by mid-Monday in Toronto, valuing the company at nearly C$206 million.\nLarge diamond sales\nSales to Belgian manufacturer HB Trading, which buys Karowe’s gem and near-gem diamonds weighing more than 10.8 carats, jumped 28% to $48.5 million. The arrangement accounted for 79% of quarterly revenue.\nRevenue from regular tenders held at $11.2 million as higher sales volumes offset an 11% decline in average prices. Revenue from Lucara’s diamond sales through the Clara digital platform fell 30% to $1.4 million, largely reflecting a 26% drop in average prices per carat.\nThe lower Clara prices reflected a greater proportion of smaller, lower-quality diamonds from stockpiled ore and continued pricing pressure on smaller goods, Lucara said.\nLucara sold Clara to a group led by former CEO Eira Thomas and HRA Group in 2024, while entering a five-year rough-diamond supply agreement that allowed termination after its second anniversary. Lucara has given notice to end the agreement on Dec. 22 and will shift those sales to regular tenders in the first quarter of 2027.\nKarowe expansion\nTotal production declined 0.9% to 96,767 carats, comprising 94,067 carats from direct ore feed and 2,700 carats recovered from historical tailings. Alongside the 1,303-carat stone, recoveries included a 316-carat diamond and seven stones weighing between 100 and 200 carats.\nLucara lowered its annual operating-cost forecast to $24 to $26 per tonne processed, from $27.50 to $31. Quarterly costs were $22.41 per tonne, below BMO’s $29 estimate, as higher throughput and lower in-pit mining activity offset increased power costs.\nThe underground expansion moved from shaft construction to lateral development during the quarter. Lucara spent $40.7 million on the project, with $245.2 million remaining to complete it at Sept. 30, including contingency.\nNaseem Lahri, managing director of Lucara’s Botswana operations, will leave that role on Nov. 30. She will continue supporting the transition through the end of the first quarter of 2027.\nMarket outlook\nGlobal natural diamond production is forecast to fall below 100 million carats in 2026, compared with more than 150 million carats less than a decade ago, as ageing mines become depleted and producers suspend or close operations, Lucara said.\nLarger, rarer stones have shown stronger price performance, supported by constrained supply and resilient demand, while smaller commercial diamonds remain under pressure from economic uncertainty and competition from lab-grown alternatives, the company said. Synthetic diamond prices have continued to decline as production capacity expands, it added.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Diamond\nREGION: Africa Canada Europe\nCOMPANY: Lucara Diamond\nRepublish this article",
